        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"hands on steering wheel in a BMW\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"Photo: NurPhoto via Getty Images\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/gettyimages-2247409540.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You hear it all the time: Modern cars are basically smartphones on wheels. And just like the phone in your pocket, the car in your driveway collects vast amounts of data - tracking where you drive, how fast you accelerate, how hard you brake, how aggressively you turn, and much more.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The legality of this data harvesting remains hotly debated. Last year, <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2025/1/16/24345470/gm-banned-selling-driving-data-insurance-ftc\">the Federal Trade Commission penalized General Motors</a> for illegally collecting and selling precise location and driving behavior",
